    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Dealing with those risks must have been a huge part of the day-to-day reality for them. Do you think there were any specific cultural or social structures that helped facilitate this trust between merchants who might never meet, or were they mostly just relying on these formal documents?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Trust on the Silk Road was a blend of formal systems and deeply ingrained social structures, which is a fascinating point for your students. While bills of exchange provided a legal framework, the real glue was often communal and cultural. Merchants frequently relied on established diasporic communities—groups of traders from the same region who settled in foreign cities. These communities acted as informal guarantors, vouching for each other's integrity. They operated under shared customs and kinship ties, which were far more reliable than a distant imperial decree. This reliance on shared identity and reputation was crucial for bridging the vast cultural gaps between East and West.",
      "source": "target",
